Chapter 3
Sales mix, and why several products have no single breakeven
مزيج المبيعات ولماذا لا توجد نقطة تعادل واحدة لعدة منتجات
Orontes does not sell one thing. It sells tahini in jars, halva in bars and sesame paste in bulk drums, and each of the three carries a different contribution margin. The breakeven of Chapter 1 assumed a single product, and the moment a second one appears the question changes: breakeven in what?
The answer is that a multi-product company has a breakeven for every mix it might sell, and only an assumed mix produces a single number. That is not a defect of the model; it is a fact about the business, and the exam tests whether a candidate can both compute the number under an assumed mix and explain why the number moves when the mix does.
Figure F703-01 is your map.
Figure F703-01. Chapter 3 roadmap: three products, one weighted margin, and a breakeven that moves.
