WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 1 computed Orontes's breakeven on the tahini jar alone: fixed costs of $12,624,000 over a contribution margin of $4.80 gives 2,630,000 jars. Book 1 found that a change in product mix is one of the four reasons a gross margin moves, and the one candidates forget. This chapter is that reason, measured.
3.1  Three products, three margins
Figure F703-02 sets out Orontes's range.
Figure F703-02. Orontes Foods, Inc. — the three lines
@table
Product | Selling price | Variable cost | Contribution margin | Contribution ratio
Tahini, 1 kg jar | 12.00 | 7.20 | 4.80 | 40.0%
Halva bar | 3.00 | 1.80 | 1.20 | 40.0%
Sesame paste, 10 kg drum | 60.00 | 39.00 | 21.00 | 35.0%
@end
Two of the three earn the same ratio and very different amounts. A jar contributes four times what a bar does, and a drum contributes four times a jar. So a company that sells more drums earns more contribution for each unit sold, and a company that sells more bars earns less — before any question of ratio arises.
The drum, meanwhile, has the lowest ratio of the three: 21.00 ÷ 60.00 = 35.0 per cent against 40.0 for the other two. So a company that sells more drums earns less contribution for each dollar of sales.
Those two sentences point in opposite directions, and keeping them apart is the whole of this chapter. Which one matters depends on what is scarce.
If capacity is measured in units — jars filled an hour, drums handled a day — then contribution per unit is the ranking that counts, and the drum is the best product.
If the constraint is the market rather than the factory, so that the question is how much revenue the company can win, then contribution per sales dollar is the ranking that counts, and the jar and the bar are better.
If some other resource is scarce — kilograms of sesame, hours on the roaster, square metres of warehouse — then neither ranking is right, and the correct one is contribution per unit of that resource. Chapter 6 develops that properly.
