3.3  When the mix shifts
The reason the exam insists there is no unique breakeven is that the whole calculation above rests on an assumption nobody controls: that customers will go on buying in the same proportions.
Suppose they do not. Suppose the bars sell better and the jars worse, so the mix becomes four jars and twelve bars to each drum.
The bundle's contribution becomes 4 × 4.80 = 19.20 from the jars, 12 × 1.20 = 14.40 from the bars and 21.00 from the drum, which is 19.20 + 14.40 + 21.00 = 54.60.
Its revenue becomes 4 × 12.00 = 48.00, 12 × 3.00 = 36.00 and 60.00, which is 48.00 + 36.00 + 60.00 = 144.00.
The weighted ratio becomes 54.60 ÷ 144.00 = 37.92 per cent, barely moved, and the breakeven in bundles becomes 12,624,000 ÷ 54.60 = 231,209 — nearly ten thousand bundles more than before.
Nothing about prices, costs or fixed costs changed. Customers bought one more bar and one less jar per bundle, and the company must now sell more to stand still.
Figure F703-04 shows the effect across four mixes.
Figure F703-04. The same products, four mixes
@table
Mix of jars : bars : drums | Bundle contribution | Bundle revenue | Weighted ratio | Breakeven in bundles
5 : 10 : 1 | 57.00 | 150.00 | 38.0% | 221,474
4 : 12 : 1 | 54.60 | 144.00 | 37.92% | 231,209
6 : 8 : 1 | 59.40 | 156.00 | 38.08% | 212,525
3 : 10 : 2 | 68.40 | 186.00 | 36.77% | 184,561
@end
Read the last two columns together and the lesson arrives. The weighted ratio barely moves across all four mixes — from 36.77 to 38.08 per cent — while the breakeven in bundles moves by more than twenty per cent. That is because a bundle is a different thing in each row: the fourth mix contains two drums, so each bundle is larger and fewer are needed.
Which is why the answer to "what is the breakeven" is always "in what mix", and why a question that gives a sales mix is giving an assumption rather than a fact.
Three practical consequences, all examinable.
A company can miss its profit target while beating its sales target, if the mix moves towards the thinner products. The sales force is told to sell dollars and is paid on dollars, and the dollars arrive while the contribution does not.
A company can beat its profit target while missing its sales target, by the same mechanism in reverse. Book 1's warning about judging a year by its top line applies here with a number attached.
And a commission scheme paid on revenue produces exactly the first outcome. A scheme paid on contribution margin aligns the sales force with the company, and is the standard recommendation in an exam answer on this topic.
