3.4  Why there is no unique breakeven
The outcome asks for the explanation and not only the calculation, so here it is in its strongest form.
Breaking even means covering $12,624,000 of fixed costs with contribution. Any combination of jars, bars and drums whose contributions add to that figure will do it, and there are infinitely many such combinations.
Selling nothing but jars, the company needs 12,624,000 ÷ 4.80 = 2,630,000 jars, which is $31,560,000 of sales.
Selling nothing but bars, it needs 12,624,000 ÷ 1.20 = 10,520,000 bars, which is also $31,560,000 of sales — the same, because the two products share a 40 per cent ratio.
Selling nothing but drums, it needs 12,624,000 ÷ 21.00 = 601,143 drums, which is 601,143 × 60.00 = 36,068,580 of sales.
So the breakeven in dollars is anywhere between about $31,560,000 and 36,068,580, depending entirely on what the company sells. There is no single number, and the one computed in section 3.2 is the number for one assumed mix.
That is the answer the exam wants, and the way to write it is in three sentences: the breakeven depends on the weighted contribution margin; the weighted margin depends on the mix; therefore each mix has its own breakeven, and a unique figure exists only when the mix is assumed constant.
Figure F703-05 sets out the range.
Figure F703-05. Orontes's breakeven, under four pure and mixed cases
@table
What the company sells | Units needed | Sales needed | Weighted ratio
Jars only | 2,630,000 jars | 31,560,000 | 40.0%
Bars only | 10,520,000 bars | 31,560,000 | 40.0%
Drums only | 601,143 drums | 36,068,580 | 35.0%
The 5 : 10 : 1 mix | 221,474 bundles | 33,221,100 | 38.0%
@end
One more point completes the outcome, and it is the one that connects this chapter to Book 1. A company with several products has a gross margin that moves when the mix moves, with no change in any product's own margin — which is the third of the four causes Book 1 listed for a margin change, and the one it said candidates forget. Orontes's gross margin fell from 37.0 to 35.0 per cent over three years. Part of that could be price and part could be input costs, and part could be nothing more than drums growing faster than jars. The company's own management accounts would show which; the published statements never will.
