Chapter summary
Figure F703-07 brings the chapter together.
Figure F703-07. Chapter 3 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Orontes sells a jar contributing $4.80 at a 40 per cent ratio, a bar contributing $1.20 at the same ratio and a drum contributing $21.00 at 35 per cent, so the drum is the best product per unit and the worst per sales dollar — and which ranking matters depends on what is scarce. With a mix assumed, the products become one composite bundle: five jars, ten bars and a drum contribute 24.00 + 12.00 + 21.00 = 57.00 on revenue of 150.00, a weighted ratio of 57.00 ÷ 150.00 = 38.0 per cent, so the breakeven is 12,624,000 ÷ 57.00 = 221,474 bundles, or 33,221,100 of sales against the $31,560,000 the flagship product alone suggested. Move the mix to four jars and twelve bars and the bundle contributes 54.60, taking the breakeven to 12,624,000 ÷ 54.60 = 231,209 bundles with no change in any price or cost — which is how a company beats its sales target and misses its profit target, and why a commission paid on revenue is the wrong scheme. And there is no unique breakeven at all: jars only needs $31,560,000, drums only needs 36,068,580, and every mix in between has its own answer, which is also the third of Book 1's four reasons a gross margin moves.
