Case-style practice: the sales director's bonus
Orontes's sales director is paid a bonus on revenue growth. Last year revenue grew 6 per cent and operating income fell 4 per cent. The three products are the jar at a $4.80 contribution, the bar at $1.20 and the drum at $21.00, and the mix in units moved from 5 : 10 : 1 to 4 : 14 : 1. Fixed costs were unchanged at $12,624,000.
Required. Explain, with figures, how both results can be true at once. Say what you would change about the bonus and what you would ask the sales director before changing anything. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC3-1  Answer A. 21.00 ÷ 60.00 = 35.0 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the ratio on the jar and the bar.
C is wrong. That is the contribution in dollars.
D is wrong. That is the variable cost ratio.
SC3-2  Answer A. The scarce resource decides the ranking.
B is wrong. Price says nothing about cost.
C is wrong. The ratio ranks by sales dollars, not by capacity.
D is wrong. Gross margin contains fixed factory costs.
SC3-3  Answer A. A large contribution on a large price can be a small ratio.
B is wrong. The price alone is not the point.
C is wrong. Every product here has variable costs.
D is wrong. Fixed costs are not attributed per unit in this analysis.
SC3-4  Answer A. 24.00 + 12.00 + 21.00 = 57.00.
B is wrong. This omits a component.
C is wrong. That is the bundle's revenue.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
SC3-5  Answer A. 12,624,000 ÷ 57.00 = 221,474 bundles.
B is wrong. The answer is in bundles, not jars.
C is wrong. That is the single-product answer.
D is wrong. That is the breakeven in dollars.
SC3-6  Answer A. A weighted average lies between its extremes.
B is wrong. No weighting can exceed the highest component.
C is wrong. Nor fall below the lowest.
D is wrong. The simple average ignores the weights.
SC3-7  Answer A. A lower weighted margin needs more volume.
B is wrong. The direction is the opposite.
C is wrong. The breakeven moves with the weighted margin.
D is wrong. Fixed costs are unaffected by mix.
SC3-8  Answer A. Dollars arrive and contribution does not.
B is wrong. Lower fixed costs would raise profit.
C is wrong. A higher ratio would raise profit.
D is wrong. Tax affects net income, not operating income.
SC3-9  Answer A. Revenue-based pay ignores margin.
B is wrong. Alignment requires paying on contribution.
C is wrong. No standard governs commissions.
D is wrong. Incentives change behaviour, which is the point.
SC3-10  Answer A. 12,624,000 ÷ 4.80 = 2,630,000 jars at 12.00 each.
B is wrong. That is the bundle figure.
C is wrong. That is the drums-only figure.
D is wrong. That is the bars-only figure.
SC3-11  Answer A. Each mix has its own weighted margin.
B is wrong. Fixed costs are given.
C is wrong. Prices are held constant in the analysis.
D is wrong. Tax does not affect the breakeven.
SC3-12  Answer A. Mix is the cause that leaves every individual margin intact.
B is wrong. Fixed costs are below the gross margin in a contribution analysis and do not move it.
C is wrong. Volume changes the amount, not the rate.
D is wrong. Tax is below the line entirely.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Contribution per unit, product A | 10.00: 25.00 − 15.00 = 10.00.
Contribution per unit, product B | 3.00: 10.00 − 7.00 = 3.00.
Bundle contribution | 29.00: 2 × 10.00 = 20.00 from A and 3 × 3.00 = 9.00 from B.
Bundle revenue | 80.00: 2 × 25.00 = 50.00 and 3 × 10.00 = 30.00.
Weighted contribution ratio | 36.25 per cent: 29.00 ÷ 80.00 = 36.25 per cent.
Breakeven in bundles | 40,000: fixed costs of 1,160,000 divided by the bundle contribution give 40,000 bundles.
Units of each at breakeven | 80,000 of A and 120,000 of B: 2 × 40,000 = 80,000 and 3 × 40,000 = 120,000.
What it tells you | The breakeven in sales is 40,000 × 80.00 = 3,200,000, and it would be 1,160,000 ÷ 0.40 = 2,900,000 if the company sold nothing but product A. The 300,000 of difference is the cost of carrying the thinner product, and it is the number to weigh against whatever the thinner product brings in volume or in customers.
@end
