Case-style set: the sales director's bonus
How both can be true. Under the old mix of 5 : 10 : 1, a bundle contributed 24.00 + 12.00 + 21.00 = 57.00 on revenue of 150.00, a ratio of 38.0 per cent. Under the new mix of 4 : 14 : 1 it contributes 4 × 4.80 = 19.20 from jars, 14 × 1.20 = 16.80 from bars and 21.00 from the drum, which is 19.20 + 16.80 + 21.00 = 57.00 — the same contribution — on revenue of 48.00 + 42.00 + 60.00 = 150.00, also the same. On those figures contribution per bundle is unchanged, so the fall in operating income must come from the bundles themselves: revenue grew 6 per cent while the bundle got no richer, and contribution grew by less than the fixed costs' share of the extra activity absorbed.
The general mechanism is the one to state. Revenue is a count of dollars and contribution is a count of margin, and a mix that moves towards bars buys dollars with the thinnest product in the range. A bar brings $3.00 of revenue and $1.20 of contribution; a jar brings $12.00 and $4.80. Four bars equal one jar in revenue and in contribution — but only if the four bars cost no more to sell, pick, pack and deliver than the one jar, which they certainly do.
What I would change. Pay the bonus on contribution margin rather than on revenue, and state the margin per product in the sales system so that the sales force sees what it is earning for the company.
What I would ask first. Whether the mix shift was the sales director's doing or the market's. If customers are moving to bars, the bonus is not the problem and the product range is. If the sales force chose the easy sale, the bonus is the problem and changing it will change the behaviour — and the second question is what the per-unit cost of handling a bar really is, because the figures above assume it is proportionate and nobody has checked.
